          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            String theory is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            in fundamental
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+everything"} theory of everything
            in the sense of fundamental physics, but the jury on that is still out.
          %p
            Below we indicate the basic idea and provide pointers to further details. See also the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+FAQ"} string theory FAQ
          %h3#conceptually_from_qft_worldline_formalism Conceptually: From QFT worldline formalism
          %p
            %em Perturbative string theory
            is something at least close to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorification
            of the following description of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in terms of sums over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
          %p
            Recall that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            one approach to make sense of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
                expansion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
            of certain numbers assigned to each
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
          %p
            The graphs are called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %em
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"}> renormalized
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            and the sum over graphs of (renormalized) amplitudes is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
          %p
            The amplitude assigned to a single graph with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            external edges is interpreted as the amplitude for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            “quanta” or “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"}> particles
            ” of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            in question to interact in the way indicated by the graph.
          %p
            Crucial for the motivation of the idea of string theory is the observation that this (renormalized) amplitude assigned to a graph is itself the
            %em correlator
            of a 1-dimensional quantum field theory on that graph: the “worldline quantum field theory” describing the (relativistic) quantum mechanics of these particles. This is usually a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            with parameter space the given graph and target space the spacetime on which the fields live for which the perturbation series computes the path integral.
          %p
            When made explicit this is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            for computing the quantum field perturbation series. (See there for more details.)
          %p
            The premise of perturbative string theory is to replace the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            over correlators of a 1-d QFT over graphs by a sum of correlators of a 2-dQFT over 2-dimensional surfaces – called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            and hence produce an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            this way. Again in simple cases this 2d QFT is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            whose target is the spacetime in which one computes interactions.
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/StringFeynmanDiagrams.png", :width => "300"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "https://benjaminjurke.com/content/articles/2010/string-theory/"} Jurke 10
          %p
            In analogy to the previous case, one thinks of the amplitude assigned this way to a surface as the amplitude for the boundary arcs – the
            = succeed "s" do
              %em string
          %p Some of the motivations for considering this replacement of graphs by surfaces have been the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %p the 2-d correlators are better behaved in that they don’t have to be renormalized. The “counterterms” appearing in renormalization of ordinary QFT can be identified with contributions to the correlators that come from the linear extension of the strings (see the above reference for more on this);
            %li
              %p there are fewer choices involved: a Feynman graph is really a decorated graph with the decoration from some more or less arbitrary index set, describing the nature of the particles associated with a given edge and the nature of the interaction associated with a given vertex. In the sum over surfaces there is no extra decoration (except on the boundary of the surfaces) and one finds that instead a single string diagram (a 2d QFT correlator for a given surface) encodes already a sum over (infinitely) many particle species decorations and all possible interaction decorations for them;
            %li
              %p
                while there are fewer choices to be made by hand, it turns out that the effective particle content that does arise automatically from this prescription happens to be structurally of the kind one would hope for: the massless effective particles described by the string perturbation series happen to be
                = succeed "," do
                  %em gauge bosons
                %em fermions
                charged under them, and, notably,
                = succeed "s." do
                  %em graviton
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
                input of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
                combined with perturbative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
                that one would hope to see.
          %p These aspects have motivated the impression that the string perturbation series might be considerably closer to the true formalism of fundamental physics than ordinary perturbative quantum field theory. This impression is however offset by the following problems:
          %ul
            %li
              %p while the worldsheet 2d QFT whose correlators are summed over surfaces are themselves much easier to handle than the full target space quantum physics they are used to encode, a fully complete and rigorous theory of 2d QFT is available only in simple special cases.
            %li
              %p
                In particular, even though there are fewer arbitrary choices involved in the string perturbation series as compared to the ordinary Feynman perturbation series, one crucial choice still present is that of this worldsheet 2d QFT. By the above, every choice of worldsheet QFT (called a choice of “vacuum”) corresponds to a choice of effective target space geometry (to be thought of as the one that the perturbation series computes the quantum perturbations about) and particle content (see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
                for more on that). One would therefore like to understand the space of all worldsheet QFTs whose effective target space geometry and particle content is close to the one experimentally observed. After many years of rather naïve approaches to handle or not to handle this, it has more recently at least come to the general attention that there is something to be better understood here.
            %li
              %p
                More fundamentally, already the role of the original perturbation series in quantum field theory is actually not fully understood. Its main success is the observation that truncating or resumming the perturbation series in a more or less ad hoc way, it does yield values that very well describe a plethora of real world measurements. One imagines that there is a
                %em non-perturbative
                definition of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                such that in certain well-defined circumstances the perturbation series does yield an approximation to it and is
                %em a posteriori
                justified. If so, there should be an analogous nonperturbative definition of string theory. There is a large ratio of speculations as to what that might be over solid results about it.
          %h3#IdeaPhenomenology Phenomenologically
          %p
            While therefore the premise of perturbative string theory is conceptually suggestive for various reasons, there is to date no connection to experimental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            (apart from the fact that conceptual insights into string theory have helped analyze quantum field theoretic data, see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            (if done well, at least), exploring the general theory space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            and their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completions"} UV-completions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model building
            (though there is no lack of trying), where it remains very speculative. This has led to public or semi-public debates about the value of string theory for actual physics. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/criticism+of+string+theory"} criticism of string theory
            for pointers.
          %h3#Scales Scales and (no) parameters
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+tension"} string tension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
